  "_style": "Address the user as “ianao” (anao, -nao: “ny toe-ponao”) in standard official Malagasy (Malagasy ofisialy, no dialect words), warm and simple, never cheesy or overly poetic. For the couple use “ianareo roa” (you two, -nareo) or, when the app speaks as the couple, inclusive “isika / -ntsika” (“Ny hazontsika”); never the exclusive “izahay” for the couple. The partner is “ny olon-tianao” (the one you love); in short labels “olon-tiana”; never vady/vadinao (spouse), sakaiza, rangahy/ramatoa. Malagasy pronouns (izy, azy, -ny) are already gender-neutral, so keep it that way: no gendered nouns (lehilahy, vehivavy, zazalahy) and no titles like Ra-/Andriamatoa. A partner's name placeholder (%@) is never given a suffix or genitive bound to it (never “%@-ny” or “%@nao”); rephrase with “an'i %@” / “i %@” as separate words or restructure (“Nandefa taratasy i %@”, “Hazo avy amin'i %@”, “Miandry an'i %@”). Quote marks: curly “ ” outside, ‘ ’ inside; apostrophe as in the source spelling (an'ny, amin'ny) using the straight ' consistently; ellipsis as one character …; no space before ? ! : ; and one space after; keep “ · ”, \\n, emoji and every %@ / %lld / %1$@ placeholder intact and in a natural position. Numbers: space as thousands separator (1 000), comma for decimals (2,5); dates day-month-year with Malagasy month names in lowercase (14 novambra 2026), weekdays lowercase (alatsinainy), 24-hour time (14:30); “andro” for day(s), unchanged in plural. Buttons are short imperatives without “azafady” (Tehirizo, Alefa, Andramo indray, Fafao, Avereno ny fividianana); titles, tabs, buttons and chips use sentence case: capital only on the first word and proper nouns (“Fanontanian'ny andro”), keep “Coupley”, “Premium”, “CoupleSync” and “AI” unchanged. Script: Latin alphabet only (a–z, plus ô and the accents à, è, ì, ñ only where standard spelling uses them; normally none); hyphenated compounds per standard spelling (toe-po, olon-tiana, dokam-barotra). Malagasy nouns have no plural inflection: plural categories are [\"one\",\"other\"] and both usually carry the same text (“%lld andro”), adding “ireo” only when it reads better.",
